-- §13.18 (issue #18 part 2): std fds + un-magicked say + sayfd. The
-- std fds Stdin/Stdout/Stderr are preregistered as ordinary fd-table
-- entries; say is no longer magic — it writes its line through the fd
-- named by the say-fd route table (default Stdout), and fread Stdin
-- BLOCKS for a line. Run with input piped:
--
--   lindana examples/stdio.lind < examples/stdio-input.txt
--
-- Stdout output is deterministic given the input; the rerouted line
-- lands in stdio-out.tmp (created in the run's working directory,
-- like files.lind's).
--
-- Every consuming machine is gated on the completion tuple of the
-- effect that produced its data (§14): (Fread, Stdin) lands
-- atomically with the side-table write, (Fopen, Log) with the fd-table
-- entry — so the says always see the handles they name, whatever the
-- effect queue's order (§11.13 chaos). An in-transaction `out` (the
-- old (Read,)/(Routed,) shape) is NOT such a gate: the effect queued
-- next to it has not run yet.

: bytesBind Greeting "Hello, std fds!\n"
(Bytes, Greeting) : [fwrite Stdout Greeting; (Go,)]
-- fread Stdin blocks the effect runner for one line; the line lands
-- in the side-table under the same handle.
(Go,) : fread Stdin
(Fread, Stdin) : [say "you typed: %b" Stdin; (Route,)]
-- The say-FD reroute sister effect: repoint what fd Global's says go
-- through (in-transaction install, last update wins). The next say
-- lands in the file; the second sayfd reroutes back.
(Route,) : fopen Log "stdio-out.tmp" W
(Fopen, Log) : [sayfd Global Log; say "this line is rerouted"
               ; sayfd Global Stdout; (Fin,)]
(Fin,) : [say "back on stdout (last update wins)"; exit 0]
